Falsifies `paging::sv39::index_is_always_in_bounds`.

The defect: the mask is gone, so the index is every bit of the address from `shift` upward
rather than the nine that name an entry. It reads as harmless because at the *last* level the
shift is 12 and a page-aligned 39-bit VA has little left above it, and it is not: at level 0 on
Sv39 the shift is 30 and everything in bits 63:30 lands in the index, including the
sign-extension bits that are not part of the translation at all.

Expected to fail: `assert!(Sv39::index(va, level) < crate::ENTRIES)`, for any `va` with a bit
set above the nine the field should have covered. Nothing else is in the harness body, so
nothing else can fail first.

**The index math is shared and the proof is not.** `PageFormat::index` is one default method in
`lib.rs`; the `aarch64` record widens the mask to ten bits and the `x86_64` one shrinks the
table under it, so the three patches break the same line in three ways.

diff --git a/crates/paging/src/lib.rs b/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
--- a/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
@@ -478,7 +478,7 @@ pub trait PageFormat {
     /// [`LEVELS`](Self::LEVELS) so it is right for both a 4- and a 3-level walk.
     fn index(va: u64, level: usize) -> usize {
         let shift = 9 * (Self::LEVELS - 1 - level) as u32 + 12;
-        ((va >> shift) & 0x1ff) as usize
+        (va >> shift) as usize
     }
 
     /// Does `va` lie in `half` for this format? The top bits (above [`SPLIT_SHIFT`](Self::SPLIT_SHIFT))
